######################################################################
### PARLEY: THE NEGOTIATING TABLE - the negotiation table
###
### This file is registered additively from
###   gui/scripted_widgets/tnt_diplomacy.txt  ->  gui/tnt_diplomacy_window.gui = tnt_diplomacy_window
### WITHOUT that one line the window is created by nobody and is SILENTLY
### INVISIBLE. The right-hand side of that line is the name = "..." below,
### not a type name (proven by MOD\Warcraft-Guardians-of-Azeroth-2-dev:
### gui/raise_dead_widget.gui = widget_raise_dead).
###
### Reusable types live in gui/tnt_types.gui and gui/tnt_panels.gui (both
### types-only, both need no registration of their own).
### Macros (Tnt*) live in data_binding/tnt_macros.txt.
###
### ZERO vanilla files are overridden by this mod.
###
### v3 ITERATION 1 CHANGES IN THIS FILE
###   ITEM 9  `layer = middle` -> `layer = events`. The character window is ALSO
###        layer = middle (GAME\gui\window_character.gui:11), and two windows on
###        one layer have no defined z-order, which is why the table sometimes
###        appeared UNDER the character sheet. Full reasoning, including what
###        `events` keeps above us and why not `top`, at the property itself.
###   ADD 4  ALLIANCE AND NON-AGGRESSION ARE SINGLE MUTUAL ROWS. The four rows
###        tnt_row_alliance_p/_r and tnt_row_truce_p/_r are DELETED from the two
###        columns and replaced by two 34 px tnt_toggle_line rows in the CENTRE
###        column, filled through the new block "tnt_balance_mutual" on
###        tnt_balance_panel. One pact = one row = one variable pair
###        (tnt_alliance_p, tnt_truce_p); ticking the partner's copy used to price
###        a second, non-existent pact and subtract points.
###        tnt_alliance_r, tnt_truce_r, tnt_show_alliance_r and tnt_show_truce_r
###        now have NO reader in any .gui of this mod.
###   CAP  the selection cap is gone from script. The cap warning is silenced AT
###        THE SOURCE: the derived types in gui\tnt_panel_multiselect.gui no
###        longer fill block "tnt_multi_cap_visible" at all (the type's default
###        is hidden). The instance-level `visible = no` overrides this file
###        used to carry were DELETED - they never worked, see the note at the
###        six instantiations below. The domain counters in the centre card
###        became the first two lines of that card (tnt_panels.gui) - they are
###        now the only signal that a big gift is a bad idea.
###   The frozen row index table below is NOT renumbered: 3 and 4 stay reserved.
###
### v2 BUILD 2 CHANGES IN THIS FILE
###   NEW  HIDE UNAVAILABLE ROWS (the user's own new request). All 28 gateable
###        rows now carry `visible = "[TntRow('tnt_show_<item>_<side>')]"`, a
###        plain two-map-lookup variable read of the answers author:gating
###        computes ONCE at window open. NEVER a GetScriptedGui - Law R11, and 28
###        rows x every frame is exactly the shape that rule exists to forbid.
###        The 15th item, INFLUENCE, has no tnt_show_ pair on purpose: it is
###        already gated on tnt_influence_ok_value, which asks a harder question
###        (both governments must hold influence), and gating one row two ways is
###        two ways for it to disappear. 14 items x 2 sides = the 28 answers.
###        Hidden rows collapse because `vbox "tnt_column_rows"` carries
###        `ignoreinvisible = yes` (tnt_types.gui, type tnt_column).
###   2.2  MULTI-SELECT. The six single-object variables tnt_title_p/_r,
###        tnt_claim_p/_r, tnt_artifact_p/_r are DELETED (30-V2-DESIGN 3.1) and
###        replaced by the six selection LISTS tnt_sel_*. Those three rows per
###        side now show a count badge, and picker indices 6 / 7 / 10 host
###        tnt_panel_multi instead of the single-select tnt_picker_panel.
###   2.3  HOOK TIERS. The two hook rows are tnt_item_row_hook (not
###        tnt_item_row_toggle) and host tnt_control_hook_tier bound to
###        tnt_hook_p / tnt_hook_r as 0..3.
###   2.1  MARRIAGE EDITOR at overlay index 5 (tnt_panel_marriage). The row's
###        grey line names tnt_spouse_p/_r, not the deleted tnt_marriage_child_*.
###   2.5  VASSAL CONTRACT SHEET at overlay index 12 (tnt_panel_contract).
###   U1a  the two domain counters, in the centre column's contextual card -
###        two instances of author:multiselect's tnt_multi_domain_line inside
###        type tnt_context_card (tnt_panels.gui; a tnt_domain_counter type was
###        written there and deleted, see tnt_panels.gui section 3b).
###
### THE TWO COMPOUND-EDITOR PANELS TEST THE INDEX ONLY, NOT THE SIDE, and that is
### deliberate. Index 5 edits BOTH spouses at once (a left list and a right list
### in one panel) and index 12 edits the single contract that T11 guarantees can
### only exist on one side, so neither panel has a per-side twin. Testing only the
### index also means the panel appears whichever value an opener happens to write
### into tnt_picker_side, which removes a whole class of cross-agent mismatch. The
### nine single/multi-select panels keep their two-key index+side test unchanged.
###
### WHERE THE PANEL TYPES COME FROM. Four of the types instantiated below are NOT
### defined in this mod's gui/tnt_types.gui or gui/tnt_panels.gui - they belong to
### three other authors, under names the build-2 naming contract fixed in advance:
###   tnt_panel_marriage    gui/tnt_panel_marriage.gui     (author:marriage)
###   tnt_panel_multi       gui/tnt_panel_multiselect.gui  (author:multiselect)
###   tnt_panel_contract    gui/tnt_panel_contract.gui     (author:hooks-contracts)
###   tnt_control_hook_tier gui/tnt_panel_contract.gui     (author:hooks-contracts)
### All four are in types-only files and need no gui/scripted_widgets entry, the
### same rule tnt_types.gui and tnt_panels.gui already rely on. If one is missing
### the engine cannot resolve its type; the documented one-for-one replacements are
### the five build-1 shells in tnt_panels.gui section 5, and the hook control's
### swap recipe is written out in full at `type tnt_item_row_hook` in
### tnt_types.gui.
###
### v2 BUILD 1 CHANGES IN THIS FILE
###   3.2  window 1400x900 -> 1480 x 88%; `using = Window_Movable` replaces the
###        hand-written movable/min_dist pair; three columns instead of two.
###   3.3  every picker row gains a two-state control slot - the empty state is
###        the old button, the filled state is a content-shaped control.
###   3.4  the centre column is a full summary: scales, headline, decomposition,
###        verdict, the deal sheet and one contextual card.
###   D3   the two prisoners rows no longer hang a container off a blockoverride;
###        the list is a normal panel at picker index 8, and the hover tooltip
###        that remains is the fixed, flowcontainer-based type. BOTH ROWS AND
###        BOTH PANELS ARE NOW RETIRED (user item 7) and commented out in place;
###        the composition RULE D3 proved still governs every type in this file.
###   2.4  three new currency steppers per side: prestige, piety, influence.
###
### ROW INDEX TABLE (frozen by 10-CONTRACT R0.8, extended by 30-V2-DESIGN 3.4).
### The overlay's `visible` expressions test these numbers, so they must never be
### renumbered:
###    1 gold        2 RETIRED      3 RETIRED      4 RETIRED
###    5 marriage    6 title        7 RETIRED      8 RETIRED
###    9 hook       10 artifact    11 RETIRED     12 vassal
###   13 prestige   14 piety       15 influence
###   16 hostage    17 RETIRED     18 courtier    19 subject (V26)
### Index 2 was the yearly tribute; the mechanic was retired and its code purged.
### The index is NOT reused and NOT renumbered - the numbers are frozen.
###
### Index 8 was THE PRISONER TERMS and index 11 THE THIRD-PARTY NON-AGGRESSION
### PROMISE. Both were retired on the user's ruling (items 7 and 1). Unlike the
### tribute, their code is NOT purged - it is commented out in place behind a
### tombstone, so the decision stays legible and the mechanic can be revived
### without re-deriving it. Same standing rule either way: NEVER reused, NEVER
### renumbered.
###
### V13 USER ITEM 1 RETIRED THREE MORE: index 3 THE ALLIANCE, index 4 THE
### NON-AGGRESSION TRUCE, and index 7 THE GRANTED CLAIM (row index AND overlay
### index 7, both halves). Same treatment and the same standing rule - commented
### out in place behind a tombstone, never deleted, NEVER reused, NEVER
### renumbered. THIS ONE IS A DESIGN REFUSAL, NOT A BUG FIX: all three terms let
### a player buy at this table what vanilla deliberately gates behind investing a
### lifestyle focus - alliance management and truce-buying behind the Diplomacy
### perk defensive_negotiations_perk
### (GAME\common\character_interactions\00_alliance.txt:1184 and
### GAME\common\scripted_triggers\03_fp2_scripted_triggers.txt:732), claim
### fabrication behind Learning and a Court Chaplain
### (GAME\common\council_tasks\00_court_chaplain_tasks.txt:1088). Tombstones A, B
### and C sit on the retired blocks themselves, further down this file.
###
### V16 USER ITEM 5 RETIRED ONE MORE: index 17 THE WARD / FOSTERAGE, row index and
### overlay index alike. Same treatment and the same standing rule - commented out
### in place behind a tombstone, never deleted, NEVER reused, NEVER renumbered.
### The user's reason: too abusable on points, and the hostage term at index 16
### already covers the same immersive ground. The audit found the abuse STRUCTURAL
### rather than a matter of one number; the WARD TOMBSTONE that records the worked
### case is in common/scripted_effects/tnt_32_apply.txt, and the row tombstone sits
### on the retired _p row itself, further down this file.
###
### ITEM 19 (integration contract v1, section 3) ADDED THREE OVERLAY INDICES and
### one term with none. 16 / 17 / 18 hosted the hostage, ward and courtier panels
### of gui/tnt_panel_people.gui, both sides each; 17 is now dormant with the ward
### family retired, so only 16 and 18 are live. GRANT INDEPENDENCE gets NO
### index at all: it is a bool pair, and the fealty row already proves a bool
### needs only tnt_item_row_toggle plus a tnt_show_ gate.
### 14 and 15 stay untouched although technically free - the table above still
### names them as the PIETY and INFLUENCE row identities and it is quoted from
### four files. OVERLAY 13 is RETIRED with the captive picker, and side 1 of it -
### once RESERVED for the partner-side captive panel "the day someone builds it"
### - is released with it. Neither half is reused. (The PRESTIGE ROW keeps the
### identity 13 in the table above: it is a stepper with no panel, which is why
### the overlay half of 13 could be spent on the captive picker in the first
### place. The row lives; only the overlay slot is retired.)
###
### Overlay indices in use: 5, 6, 10, 12, 16, 17, 18.
### Still free: 1, 9, 14, 15, 19+.
### RETIRED, not free: 2, 3, 4, 7, 8, 11, 13.
######################################################################

window = {
	name = "tnt_diplomacy_window"

	# type window defaults to layer = windows_layer, which sits BELOW the HUD
	# (GAME\gui\preload\defaults.gui:216, layer table GAME\gui\shared\windows.gui:1-62).
	#
	# V3 ITEM 9 - "the deal window does not cover the character window".
	# It was `layer = middle`, and so is the character window
	# (GAME\gui\window_character.gui:11). Two windows on ONE layer have no defined
	# z-order between them, which is exactly what the user saw: sometimes his own
	# window, sometimes the character sheet on top of it.
	# The layer table (GAME\gui\shared\windows.gui:1-62) is, by priority:
	#   debug 50, confirmation 11, frontend 10, tutorial 9, top 8, events 7,
	#   middle 6, royal_court 5, hud_layer 4, windows_layer 3, bottom 2,
	#   bottom_bottom 1.
	# `events` (7) is the LEAST intrusive layer that strictly beats middle (6), and
	# it is the one chosen. What it puts us BELOW is the point of choosing it:
	#   tutorial   (9) GAME\gui\window_tutorial.gui:349      - callouts stay visible
	#   confirmation(11) GAME\gui\shared\dialogs.gui:6,
	#                    window_resign_confirmation.gui:3,
	#                    window_save_game.gui:5              - dialogs stay on top
	#   top        (8) GAME\gui\hud_top.gui:7 (the always-present top bar),
	#                  GAME\gui\interaction_menu_window.gui:20,
	#                  GAME\gui\window_combat.gui:8, window_lineage.gui:12,
	#                  jomini\encyclopedia\jomini_encyclopedia.gui:5
	# `top` would have worked too, but it would put a 1480x88% window at an
	# undefined z-order against the permanently visible top HUD bar, trading a rare
	# collision for a constant one.
	# WHAT SHARES `events`: the event popups - character_event.gui:12,
	# letter_event.gui:5, anonymous_letter_event.gui:7, duel_event.gui:7,
	# scheme_preparations_event.gui:7, big_event_window.gui:14. An event that fires
	# while the table is open is therefore at an undefined z-order against it. That
	# is the accepted cost, and the documented fallback is `layer = top` if an event
	# is ever seen buried under this window.
	# *** CORRECTED. THIS WINDOW BELONGS ON `middle`, LIKE EVERY OTHER GAME WINDOW. ***
	#
	# It was on `events`, and that single line caused three separate reported faults,
	# because the layer order is  debug, confirmation, frontend, tutorial, top,
	# events, middle, ...  - i.e. `events` is ABOVE `middle`:
	#
	#   * the interaction confirmation window is layer = middle
	#     (GAME\gui\interaction_confirmation.gui:10) and so is the matchmaker
	#     (GAME\gui\interaction_marriage.gui:14). Both therefore opened BEHIND this
	#     window. That is the whole of "the classic-marriage button does not work":
	#     the click always arrived and the screen always opened, out of sight.
	#   * event popups are layer = events, the same layer as this window was, so the
	#     deal-confirmation dilemma drew at an undefined z-order against it and lost -
	#     "the event appears behind the deal window and its option is unclickable".
	#
	# The note that used to be here named `layer = top` as the fallback. That is the
	# wrong direction: to let an event show ABOVE us we must sit LOWER, not higher.
	#
	# Being on `middle` alongside the character window is fine, because within one
	# layer draw order follows creation order and anything opened later wins - which
	# is exactly how every vanilla window opens an interaction on top of itself. The
	# character window is no longer out-layered; it is CLOSED, by close_view in
	# tnt_open_window_effect (tnt_30_effects.txt). That is the half of user item 9
	# that was assigned but never written, and it is why the layer was raised instead.
	layer = middle

	# LEGAL: a window is not a child of a layout container, so it may anchor and
	# position itself. R-LAYOUT applies to children of hbox/vbox/flowcontainer only.
	parentanchor = center
	position = { 0 0 }

	# ITEM 3.2. 1480 wide fits 452 + 12 + 470 + 12 + 452 = 1398 plus the 40+40 of
	# Window_Margins = 1478. Vanilla's widest in-game window is 1390
	# (GAME\gui\window_activity.gui:3), so this is a 90 px step, not a new class of
	# window. A percentage height is vanilla-proven - `Window_Size_CharacterList`
	# is `size = { 745 88% }`, GAME\gui\shared\windows.gui:79-82.
	# If 88% ever misbehaves, the flat fallback is size = { 1480 940 }.
	size = { 1480 88% }
	allow_outside = yes

	# Replaces the hand-written `movable = yes` + `min_dist_from_screen_edge = 200`
	# pair with the vanilla template that IS that pair
	# (template Window_Movable, GAME\gui\shared\windows.gui:573-577).
	using = Window_Movable

	alwaystransparent = no
	filter_mouse = all

	using = Window_Background
	using = Window_Decoration_Spike

	# Opened by the interaction's on_send (tnt_open_window_effect sets tnt_open = 1).
	# The typed validity guard fail-closes the whole binding subtree if the partner
	# dies or the session is invalidated, so sheet values cannot read a null scope.
	visible = "[And( TntOn('tnt_open'), TntVar('tnt_partner').Char.IsValid )]"

	# MakeScope.GetList is a persistent save-game variable list, not a live query,
	# so every picker list is rebuilt each time the window appears.
	state = {
		name = _show
		using = Animation_FadeIn_Quick
		using = Sound_WindowShow_Standard

		on_start = "[TntExec('tnt_refresh_lists')]"
	}

	state = {
		name = _hide
		using = Animation_FadeOut_Quick
		using = Sound_WindowHide_Standard
	}

	# AUTOPAUSE: REMOVED ENTIRELY, BY USER RULING ("вообще убрать автопаузу").
	# Opening this window does NOT pause the game. Three mechanisms were tried
	# across three sessions and none proved reliable:
	#   1. message auto_pause (common\messages + message_filter_types, long
	#      deleted): the pause was lost whenever the letter event's own pause
	#      channel released on close — the field is unexercised by vanilla.
	#   2. [OnPause] from a widget state's on_start (widget tnt_pause_on_open,
	#      formerly here): fired inconsistently across the two opening doors
	#      (interaction vs. AI letter).
	#   3. the tnt_pause_latch rework of that widget (a GetVariableSystem latch
	#      plus a second latcher widget and _show/_hide clears): still failed
	#      the user's third playtest — the window opened with no pause at all.
	# Verdict: not expressible reliably with the engine surface available to a
	# no-override mod. Do NOT reintroduce a pauser here or in script; the
	# script-side twin of this tombstone is tnt_30_effects.txt:73-85.

	# ESC handling. header_pattern's own close button loses its shortcut below
	# (we replace button_close_tooltip), so exactly one widget owns close_window.
	# Trick from MOD\2519175282\gui\interaction_menu_window.gui:24-29.
	# V3 ITERATION 3: the second line is the ONLY cleanup the preset overlay
	# needs. 'tnt_preset_menu' is a GUI variable, not a character variable, so
	# tnt_close_window_effect cannot and must not touch it - but it does outlive
	# the window, and a menu left open would be on screen the instant the window
	# was reopened. Every preset button and the menu's own Close already clear it.
	#
	# THIS SITE IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS, and it is the ESC handler rather than the
	# header's X for a reason: tnt_preset_overlay is declared AFTER the window's
	# main vbox, so while a menu is open it draws over the header and the footer
	# and `filter_mouse = all` swallows their clicks - the X is unreachable. A
	# keyboard `shortcut` does not care about z-order, so ESC is the only way to
	# close the window with a menu still up. The same line is repeated on the X
	# purely defensively, in case a later change reorders the overlay.
	# Stacked onclick, both lines fire: GAME\gui\hud.gui:988-989.
	button_normal = {
		name = "tnt_esc"
		size = { 0 0 }
		shortcut = "close_window"
		onclick = "[TntExec('tnt_close_window')]"
		onclick = "[GetVariableSystem.Clear( 'tnt_preset_menu' )]"
	}

	vbox = {
		using = Window_Margins
		layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
		layoutpolicy_vertical = expanding
		spacing = 6

		####################################################################
		### HEADER
		####################################################################
		header_pattern = {
			layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding

			blockoverride "header_text"
			{
				text = "tnt_window_title"
			}

			blockoverride "button_close_tooltip"
			{
				tooltip = "tnt_btn_close"
			}

			blockoverride "button_close"
			{
				onclick = "[TntExec('tnt_close_window')]"
				onclick = "[GetVariableSystem.Clear( 'tnt_preset_menu' )]"
			}
		}

		####################################################################
		### THE THREE COLUMNS
		### Left   = what the player gives   (_p variables)
		### Centre = the balance, the decomposition and the deal sheet
		### Right  = what the partner gives  (_r variables)
		### Each column's datacontext is set EXPLICITLY. Never rely on an
		### ambient Character. Proof of the .Char-as-datacontext form:
		### GAME\gui\window_situation_list.gui:1031.
		####################################################################
		hbox = {
			name = "tnt_table"
			layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
			layoutpolicy_vertical = expanding
			spacing = 12

			### V3 FIX, REVISED BY USER ITEM 17(c). V3 hid this whole hbox behind any
			### open picker (visible = Not(TntOn('tnt_picker'))) because the column
			### heads' portrait_head_small is rendered in a later pass than flat
			### widgets and punched through tnt_picker_overlay's dim. But an invisible
			### widget leaves LAYOUT, so the window snapped down to header + footer -
			### the "collapse" the user reported. The table therefore stays drawn now:
			### dimmed and click-proof under the overlay (filter_mouse = all), exactly
			### as it already behaves under tnt_preset_overlay, which dims without
			### hiding anything. Only what actually punches through is gated - the
			### column-head portrait (tnt_column, gui\tnt_types.gui) and the
			### chosen-character head (tnt_chosen_char, gui\tnt_panels.gui) - which
			### the nonwar rows were the original consumers of, until user item 1
			### retired them; the hostage rows still use it (the ward rows did too
			### until V16 user item 5 retired that family) - both gated on
			### the picker AND the preset variable, which the old table hide never
			### covered.
			###
			### The blue diagnostic marker that once sat here proved the V3 hide took
			### effect and eliminated the column head as the thing covering the
			### marriage button; the real culprit was a non-transparent caption
			### inside the panel itself.
			### FIRST-LAUNCH CHECK (mandatory): open each picker and a preset menu
			### and confirm no portrait sits above the dim - coats of arms draw flat
			### and are expected covered. Fallback if a run disproves the z-order
			### claim: restore visible = "[Not( TntOn('tnt_picker') )]" on this hbox.

			################################################################
			### LEFT COLUMN - YOU OFFER
			################################################################
			tnt_column = {
				name = "tnt_column_actor"
				datacontext = "[GetPlayer]"

				blockoverride "tnt_column_header"
				{
					text = "tnt_col_actor"
				}

				blockoverride "tnt_column_rows"
				{
					####################################################
					### HOW EVERY ROW BELOW IS GATED - read this once.
					###
					### visible = "[TntRow('tnt_show_<item>_<side>')]"
					###
					### That macro (data_binding/tnt_macros.txt) expands to
					###   Or( tnt_show_X > 0, Not( tnt_gate_live > 0 ) )
					### i.e. "show it if the gating pass said yes, OR if the gating
					### pass never ran". The second half is the safety net, and it
					### is not decoration: TntOn on an UNSET variable reads 0,
					### which means HIDE, so a bare TntOn would empty the entire
					### window the moment the gating effect was missing or the
					### integrator forgot to call it. That is defect B-3 of build 1
					### (a row gated on a variable nothing wrote, invisible in every
					### game) multiplied by 28. With tnt_gate_live absent the window
					### degrades to build 1 - every row shown - which is a
					### degradation the player can still use.
					###
					### THREE ROWS COMBINE THE GATE WITH THE GAME RULE:
					### marriage, title and vassal keep TntOn('tnt_advanced')
					### inside an And(), because the advanced-terms game rule is a
					### user preference and the gating answer is a legality fact.
					### Both must be true. And( a, b ) is vanilla
					### (GAME\gui\hud_outliner.gui:1567).
					###
					### THE INFLUENCE ROW IS THE ONE EXCEPTION and keeps only
					### TntPos('tnt_influence_ok_value') - see the file header.
					####################################################

					####################################################
					### V3 ITERATION 3, ADDITIONS 2 AND 3 - THE FOUR
					### CURRENCY ROWS ON EACH SIDE ARE
					### tnt_item_row_currency + tnt_stepper_full. (They
					### replaced a narrower tnt_item_row_stepper +
					### tnt_stepper pair, both types now deleted.)
					###
					### Each gains a COARSE pair of buttons (+/-1000, and
					### +/-250 on influence, whose own cap is 1000) and one
					### PRESET OPENER that raises the modal at the bottom of
					### this file. There is no third mouse button to bind -
					### author:script measured every .gui and .txt under GAME
					### and found zero onshiftclick / onctrlclick /
					### onaltclick / onmiddleclick sites - so the coarse tier
					### had to become its own pair of controls.
					###
					### THE PRESET OPENER IS A GUI VARIABLE, NOT A SCRIPTED
					### GUI. GetVariableSystem.Set stores the ROW KEY, and
					### that one flag both raises tnt_preset_overlay and
					### picks which of its eight panels is visible. See the
					### long note on type tnt_preset_overlay in
					### gui/tnt_types.gui for why this is not a tnt_picker
					### index: every tnt_picker index is written by a
					### scripted gui, and no scripted gui opens a preset
					### menu, so reusing an index would have meant inventing
					### an identifier (R2).
					####################################################

					### 1 - GOLD (fine LMB +/-10 RMB +/-100, coarse +/-1000, presets)
					tnt_item_row_currency = {
						name = "tnt_row_gold_p"
						blockoverride "tnt_row_interest" {
							tnt_interest_badge = {
								blockoverride "tnt_interest_state" { visible = "[TntPos('tnt_interests_enabled_value')]" }
								blockoverride "tnt_interest_value" { fontcolor = "[TntInterestColor('tnt_interest_gold_p_badge_percent_value')]" raw_text = "[TntSV('tnt_interest_gold_p_badge_percent_value')|0]%" }
								blockoverride "tnt_interest_tooltip" {
									tooltipwidget = {
										tnt_interest_tooltip = {
											blockoverride "tnt_interest_heading" { text = "tnt_interest_receive" }
